# Cross-platform line-ending policy.
#
# Most of this team is on macOS/Linux; a couple of us are on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true. Without this file, a Windows checkout can commit CRLF into
# files the rest of the team then cannot run. That already happened:
# quest_app/run.sh was stored with 126 CRLF lines, which fails on macOS with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" - the deploy script simply would not start.
#
# Text is stored LF in the repository no matter what the working tree looks like.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are LF, always. A CRLF shebang is fatal on macOS/Linux.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF - PowerShell 5.1 is happier with it, and these
# are never executed on macOS/Linux.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never apply EOL conversion. Git's NUL-byte heuristic usually
# classifies these correctly, but a .glb or an animation sidecar .bin silently
# mangled by a line-ending rewrite is unrecoverable and would not be obvious
# until a model failed to load on device. State it rather than infer it.
*.glb   binary
*.bin   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.wav   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.so    binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.apk   binary
*.keystore binary

# .gltf is JSON, but it is generated - leave its bytes alone rather than
# normalising a file no human edits.
*.gltf  -text
